/* Documentation → Getting connected, Connection section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"The card at the top names your computer once OBS connects, with its OBS version and whether it came in over USB or Wi-Fi. While OBS is connected and the camera is idle, its **Start** button starts the stream from here; otherwise the card shows the phone's address, which is what OBS needs." = "The card at the top names your computer once OBS connects, with its OBS version and whether it came in over USB or Wi-Fi. While OBS is connected and the camera is idle, its **Start** button starts the stream from here; otherwise the card shows the phone's address, which is what OBS needs.";

/* Documentation section header on the Getting connected page */
"Connection" = "Connection";

/* Documentation → Getting connected, Format section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Format** is one row — resolution, frame rate, codec and color — with the choices behind it. Resolution and frame rate offer only what the chosen camera supports. Codec and Color constrain each other: HDR and Apple Log are HEVC only, so picking H.264 returns Color to Standard and picking HDR or Log switches the codec to HEVC." = "**Format** is one row — resolution, frame rate, codec and color — with the choices behind it. Resolution and frame rate offer only what the chosen camera supports. Codec and Color constrain each other: HDR and Apple Log are HEVC only, so picking H.264 returns Color to Standard and picking HDR or Log switches the codec to HEVC.";

/* Documentation → Getting connected, Quality section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Quality**, in the Format sheet. **Balanced** streams at a bitrate that is safe on ordinary Wi-Fi and only backs off from it. **Maximum** starts higher and keeps probing upward while the connection stays clean — up to about six times the balanced rate over USB, four over Wi-Fi — backing off the moment frames queue or drop and settling just under whatever the link carries. It also switches the encoder to quality-first settings and, unless system video effects are allowed, streams from the full sensor readout rather than the binned format. More data and more heat; watch the Stats line the first time." = "**Quality**, in the Format sheet. **Balanced** streams at a bitrate that is safe on ordinary Wi-Fi and only backs off from it. **Maximum** starts higher and keeps probing upward while the connection stays clean — up to about six times the balanced rate over USB, four over Wi-Fi — backing off the moment frames queue or drop and settling just under whatever the link carries. It also switches the encoder to quality-first settings and, unless system video effects are allowed, streams from the full sensor readout rather than the binned format. More data and more heat; watch the Stats line the first time.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Color section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**HDR (HLG)** streams 10-bit color. OBS tone-maps it for SDR scenes, and HDR canvases get the real thing." = "**HDR (HLG)** streams 10-bit color. OBS tone-maps it for SDR scenes, and HDR canvases get the real thing.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Color section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Apple Log** (Pro iPhones, iOS 17+) streams a flat 10-bit image made for grading — add an **Apply LUT** filter to the source in OBS and load an Apple Log LUT." = "**Apple Log** (Pro iPhones, iOS 17+) streams a flat 10-bit image made for grading — add an **Apply LUT** filter to the source in OBS and load an Apple Log LUT.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Color section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"Both are HEVC-only and take effect when the camera next starts. Color lives in the Format sheet and only offers what this phone can capture." = "Both are HEVC-only and take effect when the camera next starts. Color lives in the Format sheet and only offers what this phone can capture.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Green screen section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Green screen** keeps you in the picture and paints everything else solid green before the video leaves the phone — turning it on sets Color to Standard. The **LensLink Camera** source in OBS adds a ready-tuned filter that keys the green out; it's added once, so deleting or re-tuning it in OBS sticks." = "**Green screen** keeps you in the picture and paints everything else solid green before the video leaves the phone — turning it on sets Color to Standard. The **LensLink Camera** source in OBS adds a ready-tuned filter that keys the green out; it's added once, so deleting or re-tuning it in OBS sticks.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Green screen section: switching it while live (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"Mid-stream, **hold** the green screen button beside the lens buttons to turn it on or off; it lights up yellow while it's on. Switching restarts the camera for a moment." = "Mid-stream, **hold** the green screen button beside the lens buttons to turn it on or off; it lights up yellow while it's on. Switching restarts the camera for a moment.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Depth assist section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Depth assist** sharpens the cutout with real depth — the front camera on Face ID phones, or the rear Main lens on Pro (LiDAR) phones. Other lenses use shape detection alone, which keeps every person in frame. It also turns off Center Stage and the other system video effects." = "**Depth assist** sharpens the cutout with real depth — the front camera on Face ID phones, or the rear Main lens on Pro (LiDAR) phones. Other lenses use shape detection alone, which keeps every person in frame. It also turns off Center Stage and the other system video effects.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Depth assist section (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"**Subject** works while depth assist runs: anything farther from the phone than the cutoff becomes background — the way to drop a passer-by behind you. It starts on **Auto**, which keeps the cutoff about 0.6 m behind you, and an **A** on the button says so. Tap the lit green screen button beside the lens buttons for the dial: drag to set a distance, or past 5 m for **All** (no limit), and tap the button again for Auto." = "**Subject** works while depth assist runs: anything farther from the phone than the cutoff becomes background — the way to drop a passer-by behind you. It starts on **Auto**, which keeps the cutoff about 0.6 m behind you, and an **A** on the button says so. Tap the lit green screen button beside the lens buttons for the dial: drag to set a distance, or past 5 m for **All** (no limit), and tap the button again for Auto.";

/* Documentation → Camera & picture, Depth assist section, third paragraph: the Depth tags in Setup and the Format menus, and zoom hidden while depth assist runs (Markdown: keep the ** markers) */
"Where the camera and format you picked have depth assist, the Green screen row shows **Depth**, and the Format menus mark the resolutions and frame rates that have it. The camera can't zoom while depth assist runs, so the zoom dial and the **2** button step aside until it stops." = "Where the camera and format you picked have depth assist, the Green screen row shows **Depth**, and the Format menus mark the resolutions and frame rates that have it. The camera can't zoom while depth assist runs, so the zoom dial and the **2** button step aside until it stops.";

